# Environment Configuration

## Environment Types

### Production Environment

**Location:** Raspberry Pi at `192.168.1.110`

```yaml
host: 192.168.1.110
user: david
ha_config_path: /opt/homeassistant/config/
ha_runtime: docker
container_name: homeassistant
restart_command: docker restart homeassistant
```

**Characteristics:**

- Home Assistant runs in Docker container
- Configuration at `/opt/homeassistant/config/`
- Always deploy via SCP + SSH (never direct edit)
- Runs 24/7 with live automations and devices

**Access Pattern:**

```bash
# Copy files to home directory first
scp localfile.yaml david@192.168.1.110:/home/david/

# Then SSH and deploy with sudo
ssh david@192.168.1.110
sudo cp /home/david/localfile.yaml /opt/homeassistant/config/
docker restart homeassistant
```

### Development Environment

**Location:** Local Windows machine

```yaml
os: Windows 10/11
shell: PowerShell
workspace: C:\Users\david\Repos\ha_brain
ha_config_source: homeassistant-config/
```

**Characteristics:**

- Edit files locally in workspace
- Test YAML syntax locally when possible
- Use git for version control
- Deploy to production via SCP

## Shell Environment: PowerShell

### Default Shell

The agent operates in **Windows PowerShell** by default.

**Important Gotchas:**

#### 1. Path Separators

```powershell
# ✅ PowerShell accepts both
C:\Users\david\file.txt
C:/Users/david/file.txt

# But prefer backslash in PowerShell
Get-Content C:\Users\david\file.txt
```

#### 2. Command Syntax Differences

**Bash vs PowerShell:**

```powershell
# Bash: cat file.txt
# PowerShell:
Get-Content file.txt
# or short alias:
cat file.txt  # works but Get-Content is preferred

# Bash: ls -la
# PowerShell:
Get-ChildItem -Force
# or alias:
ls  # works but may have different behavior

# Bash: grep "pattern" file.txt
# PowerShell:
Select-String -Pattern "pattern" -Path file.txt

# Bash: export VAR=value
# PowerShell:
$env:VAR = "value"

# Bash: echo "text" > file.txt
# PowerShell:
"text" | Out-File file.txt
# or:
Set-Content -Path file.txt -Value "text"
```

#### 3. Quoting and Escaping

```powershell
# ❌ Single quotes don't expand variables
$name = "World"
'Hello $name'  # Outputs: Hello $name

# ✅ Double quotes expand variables
"Hello $name"  # Outputs: Hello World

# Escaping with backtick (not backslash!)
"Cost: `$100"  # Outputs: Cost: $100

# Special characters in paths
Get-Content "C:\Users\david\My Documents\file.txt"
```

#### 4. Piping and Output

```powershell
# PowerShell pipes objects, not text
Get-Process | Where-Object {$_.CPU -gt 100}

# To work with text like bash:
Get-Process | Out-String | Select-String "chrome"
```

#### 5. Exit Codes

```powershell
# Check last exit code
$LASTEXITCODE

# Bash: $?
# PowerShell: $? (but different meaning - success/fail, not exit code)
```

#### 6. Array and List Operations

```powershell
# Arrays use @()
$files = @("file1.txt", "file2.txt", "file3.txt")

# Access elements
$files[0]  # file1.txt

# Length
$files.Length  # or $files.Count
```

#### 7. Command Execution

```powershell
# Execute external commands
& "C:\Program Files\tool.exe" --arg value

# Call expressions
Invoke-Expression "Get-Process"

# Or use:
cmd /c "some-command"  # Run in cmd.exe
```

#### 8. Home Directory

```powershell
# Bash: ~/ or $HOME
# PowerShell:
$HOME  # C:\Users\david
$env:USERPROFILE  # Same as $HOME

# cd ~/ works in PowerShell
cd ~
```

### PowerShell Best Practices for Agents

1. **Use full cmdlet names in scripts** (not aliases) for clarity

   ```powershell
   # ✅ Clear
   Get-Content file.txt

   # ❌ Ambiguous in documentation
   cat file.txt
   ```

2. **Handle paths cross-platform aware**

   ```powershell
   # Use Join-Path for path construction
   $configPath = Join-Path $HOME "Repos\ha_brain\config.yaml"
   ```

3. **Check command availability**

   ```powershell
   # Check if command exists
   if (Get-Command "docker" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) {
       docker ps
   }
   ```

4. **Error handling**

   ```powershell
   # Stop on errors
   $ErrorActionPreference = "Stop"

   # Try-catch blocks
   try {
       Get-Content nonexistent.txt
   } catch {
       Write-Error "File not found: $_"
   }
   ```

5. **SSH from PowerShell**

   ```powershell
   # PowerShell has built-in SSH client (Windows 10+)
   ssh david@192.168.1.110
   scp file.txt david@192.168.1.110:/home/david/

   # No need for special quoting usually
   ssh david@192.168.1.110 "ls -la /home/david"
   ```

### Common Pitfalls

#### Pitfall 1: Command Not Found

```powershell
# ❌ Bash command doesn't exist
grep "pattern" file.txt
# Error: The term 'grep' is not recognized

# ✅ Use PowerShell equivalent
Select-String -Pattern "pattern" -Path file.txt
```

#### Pitfall 2: Path with Spaces

```powershell
# ❌ Will fail
Get-Content C:\My Documents\file.txt

# ✅ Quote the path
Get-Content "C:\My Documents\file.txt"

# ✅ Or escape spaces
Get-Content C:\My` Documents\file.txt
```

#### Pitfall 3: Line Endings

```powershell
# Windows uses CRLF (\r\n)
# Linux uses LF (\n)

# When creating files for Linux deployment:
$content | Out-File -FilePath file.txt -Encoding UTF8 -NoNewline
```

#### Pitfall 4: Execution Policy

```powershell
# Scripts may not run due to execution policy
Set-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned -Scope CurrentUser

# Or bypass for single script:
PowerShell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File script.ps1
```

## Remote Access Patterns

### SSH from PowerShell to Linux Host

```powershell
# Standard SSH (works in PowerShell)
ssh david@192.168.1.110

# SCP files
scp homeassistant-config\configuration.yaml david@192.168.1.110:/home/david/

# Execute remote command
ssh david@192.168.1.110 "docker ps"

# Multi-line remote commands (use semicolons)
ssh david@192.168.1.110 "cd /opt/homeassistant; ls -la"
```

### File Transfer Considerations

```powershell
# ✅ PowerShell to Linux via SCP
# Windows paths with backslashes work
scp C:\Users\david\Repos\ha_brain\config.yaml david@192.168.1.110:/home/david/

# ✅ Batch transfer multiple files
scp homeassistant-config\*.yaml david@192.168.1.110:/home/david/configs/

# ❌ Don't use PowerShell cmdlets for remote Linux files
# This won't work:
Copy-Item \\192.168.1.110\home\david\file.txt .\  # Not a Windows share!

# ✅ Use SCP instead:
scp david@192.168.1.110:/home/david/file.txt .\
```

## Environment Variables

### Key Variables to Know

```powershell
# User and paths
$env:USERNAME          # Current user
$env:USERPROFILE       # C:\Users\david
$env:HOMEDRIVE         # C:
$env:HOMEPATH          # \Users\david

# PowerShell specific
$PSVersionTable        # PowerShell version info
$pwd                   # Current directory (Get-Location)
$HOME                  # Home directory

# Custom for this project
$env:HA_HOST = "192.168.1.110"
$env:HA_USER = "david"
```

## Working Directory Awareness

### Typical Workflow

```powershell
# Start in workspace root
cd C:\Users\david\Repos\ha_brain

# Verify location
Get-Location  # or: pwd

# Edit local config
code homeassistant-config\configuration.yaml

# Deploy to remote
scp homeassistant-config\configuration.yaml david@192.168.1.110:/home/david/

# SSH and apply
ssh david@192.168.1.110
# Now in bash on Linux host:
sudo cp /home/david/configuration.yaml /opt/homeassistant/config/
docker restart homeassistant
exit  # Back to PowerShell
```

## Cross-Platform Compatibility

### When Writing Scripts

If a script might run on both Windows and Linux:

```powershell
# Detect OS
if ($IsWindows -or $env:OS -match "Windows") {
    # Windows-specific logic
    $separator = "\"
} else {
    # Linux-specific logic
    $separator = "/"
}

# Use Path cmdlets (cross-platform)
$configPath = Join-Path $HOME "config" "file.yaml"
```

### Git Bash vs PowerShell

Some users have Git Bash installed. If switching:

```bash
# In Git Bash (bash syntax)
export VAR=value
cat file.txt

# In PowerShell (different syntax)
$env:VAR = "value"
Get-Content file.txt
```

**Rule:** Default to PowerShell commands unless user specifies bash.
